    "evidence_text": "Cognitive behavioral therapy vs zopiclone for treatment of chronic primary insomnia in older adults: a randomized controlled trial.\n\nCONTEXT Insomnia is a common condition in older adults and is associated with a number of adverse medical, social, and psychological consequences.\n\nPrevious research has suggested beneficial outcomes of both psychological and pharmacological treatments, but blinded placebo-controlled trials comparing the effects of these treatments are lacking.   \n\n\nOBJECTIVE To examine short- and long-term clinical efficacy of cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and pharmacological treatment in older adults experiencing chronic primary insomnia.   \n\n\nRESULTS CBT resulted in improved short- and long-term outcomes compared with zopiclone on 3 out of 4 outcome measures.\n\nFor most outcomes, zopiclone did not differ from placebo.\n\nThe study used a pre-post intervention design.   \n\n\nThe intervention's effectiveness did not differ according to gender or ethnicity (black versus white).   \n\n\nCONCLUSION A brief cognitive behavioral intervention can reduce binge eating behaviors among bariatric surgery candidates.\n\nBehavioral treatment of obesity in patients encountered in primary care settings: a systematic review.\n\nNo studies were found in which primary care practitioners delivered counseling that followed the CMS guidelines.\n\nMindfulness meditation and improvement in sleep quality and daytime impairment among older adults with sleep disturbances: a randomized clinical trial.\n\nIMPORTANCE Sleep disturbances are most prevalent among older adults and often go untreated.\n\nTreatment options for sleep disturbances remain limited, and there is a need for community-accessible programs that can improve sleep.   \n\n\nBipolar disorder is a severe psychiatric disorder that is associated with persistent changes in the quality, duration and architecture of sleep.\n\nSleep is modified by various cytokines including IL-6.\n\nA mental health intervention for schoolchildren exposed to violence: a randomized controlled trial.\n\nSimilar results were obtained with chronic alprazolam treatment and with other benzodiazepine treatments.   \n\n\nData from this study on the treatment of chronic, severe insomnia (a small subset of all insomnia) are not generalizable to the typical insomnia patient.\n\nNeither treatment, however, eliminated headache problems.\n\nTreatments for somnambulism in adults: assessing the evidence.\n\nGenerally, if sleepwalking is causing distress or danger in spite of safety measures, medical or psychological treatment is indicated.\n\nClinicians will need to assess the evidence for treatment options.\n\nNo properly powered rigorous controlled trials were found for treatment of sleepwalking in adults.\n\nA clinical approach to circadian rhythm sleep disorders.\n\nDiagnostic tools such as sleep diaries and wrist activity monitoring are often useful in confirming the diagnosis.\n\nBecause behavioral and environmental factors often are involved in the development of these conditions, a multimodal approach is usually necessary.",
